Faxbot: error correction's frame size and error shape, and T.38 protection by packet category (spandsp 0.0.6;
builder ENG, brief 98).

Three changes, the spandsp steps of each in faxbot_t38_gateway.h, which the replay proof in asterisk/tests
compiles as well:

1. Frame size. With FAXBOT_ECM_FRAME=64 on a fax the built-in engine sends, its DCS asks for 64-octet
   error-correction frames (T.30 DCS bit 28) and spandsp sends them (octets_per_ecm_frame), set as the DCS
   goes out (faxbot_ecm_small_frames). spandsp 0.0.6 alone always sends 256-octet frames. A DCS without
   error correction is left alone; unset keeps 256.

2. Error shape. The real-time frame handler of 0004 also keeps what error correction did: partial pages,
   blocks and their frames, requests to send frames again and how many, the runs of neighbouring frames in a
   block's first request, the longest run, continue-to-correct requests, and the milliseconds from each partial
   page signal to the next image data (a block edge, or a round of resent frames). It goes on the channel at
   the end of the session as FAXBOT_ECM, for the dialplan's FaxFrames event (engine_learning.py reads it), with
   one NOTICE line. UDPTL marks each received packet that its redundancy recovered (AST_UDPTL_FRFLAG_REPAIRED),
   so FAXBOT_T38_RX says how T.38 arrived: packets received, recovered, and still missing (spandsp's count).
   The gateway (SSL Fax engine calls) sets FAXBOT_T38_RX too, for the FaxEngineCall event, and keeps it current
   while it runs: its session ends only after the hangup handler that sends that event.

3. T.38 protection by packet category. spandsp asks for each T.38 packet to be sent as many times as its
   category's setting (indicator, control data, end of control data, image data, end of image data); Asterisk
   ignored that count, so every packet went once. With FAXBOT_T38_PROTECT ("I.C.CE.IM.IME", 1 to 4 each;
   engine_learning.py sets it from the loss measured on the trunk) the terminal or gateway sets those counts,
   and ast_udptl_write sends the same datagram that many times with one UDPTL sequence number
   (AST_UDPTL_FRFLAG_REPEAT), as spandsp's own UDPTL test harness does; a receiver drops the copies it already
   has. Unset or malformed keeps Asterisk's behaviour.

Nothing here changes a call Faxbot did not mark, beyond keeping the record.

